---
description: "Review the uncommitted fixes of one cluster - are the issues resolved, was anything damaged"
mode: subagent
model: "anthropic/claude-opus-5-5"
thinking: high
tools: read, grep, bash
tool_loop:
  max_rounds: 80
params:
  manifest: string
  already_claimed: string = ""
system: "You are a strict post-fix reviewer. You judge whether uncommitted changes actually resolve the quality issues they claim to resolve, and whether they damaged anything else. You verify against the current working tree with your own eyes, never against claims. You are read-only - no edits, no git write commands."
---
let issue_files = read({ path: manifest })?
let stat = bash({ command: "git status --porcelain" })?
let diffstat = bash({ command: "git diff --stat | tail -30" })?
let diff = bash({ command: "git diff | head -c 60000" })?

import { FixReview } from "./quality.thetalib"

let rv: FixReview = @`FIX REVIEW — judge the uncommitted working-tree changes against this cluster's issues.

Issue files (the fixes' intent), one per line:
${issue_files}

git status --porcelain:
${stat}

git diff --stat (tail):
${diffstat}

git diff (possibly truncated — read files and rerun git yourself for anything
you need):
${diff}

Judge, in order:
1. Resolution. For each issue file: read it, then verify in the CURRENT working
   tree that the described problem is gone — the cited code removed or corrected
   AND every reference to it cleaned up (grep for the deleted identifiers).
   Collect the issue basenames you verified resolved. An issue with no
   working-tree change attributable to it was skipped, shed, or claimed
   already resolved by the fixer —
   legitimate: omit it from fixed_confirmed; by itself it is never grounds
   for ok: false (step 1b judges already-resolved claims).
1b. Already-resolved claims. The fixer claims these issues were already
   resolved upstream before the lane ran (comma-separated basenames; empty =
   none claimed):
   ${already_claimed}
   Verify each against HEAD, not the diff: re-run the issue's evidence hunt
   yourself and confirm the described problem is GONE from the current tree,
   AND that no working-tree change is attributable to that issue (a claim
   covering the fixer's own edit is false; such an issue belongs in step 1).
   When you can identify the upstream commit that resolved it (git log -S/-G,
   git log on the cited files; read-only), verify it and report it in
   already_resolved_sha. A claimed issue that still reproduces at HEAD goes
   to unconfirmed with why.
2. Collateral. Any change NOT required by an issue in this cluster? Any new
   cruft introduced? Any weakened or deleted test that guarded live behavior (a
   test deleted together with the cruft it exercised is fine when an issue's fix
   implies it)? Untracked leftover files in git status?
   Witness-flip coordination notes (every lens): when the diff re-pins or
   flips existing witness cells/tests — changes an asserted value, renames or
   deletes a cited test — grep docs/bugs/ for records citing those test
   files/cell ids: each must gain, in this same change, an appended dated
   coordination note ("### Coordination note — <date>, bug <NNNN> (<version>)"
   shape) with every pre-existing record line byte-identical. A flipped
   witness whose citing record got no note is must_fix; the appended notes
   are required by the fixer's house rule, not collateral — a per-lens
   "nothing else touched" bar treats them as part of the fix.
   For issues whose lens: is D7 the bar is: the smell is gone AND the suite is
   not weakened — a replaced assertion observes something real, a deleted test
   verifiably verified nothing and nothing cites it, helper dedupe kept every
   caller green, coverage-matrix.md / bug-doc citations still resolve, and no
   production src/ file changed for a D7 issue.
   For issues whose lens: is D9 the bar is: the change is a PURE MOVE/EXTRACT of
   exactly the ratified seam or home — run git diff --stat (the footprint)
   and git diff --color=always --color-moved=zebra (the ANSI move markers are
   the observable: moved blocks, not edited ones; fall back to reading both
   sides when a block is unmarked); every symbol a touched
   path exported still resolves for its importers (tsc green is the proof;
   spot-check two importers); no logic, name, or order-of-effects change; a new
   module has a header; for a breakdown the host's LOC decreased by about the
   seam's LOC (size-scan map before/after); for a misplacement/husk the
   declaration lives at the ratified home and nowhere else; no other host was
   touched. A behaviour edit smuggled into a move is must_fix.
   For issues whose lens: is D4 the bar is: every cited copy is gone and calls
   the one helper; the helper's body is one of the former copies verbatim (or
   the ruled drift resolution); no call site changed behaviour; the helper's
   module adds no cross-directory import edge that did not already exist in
   src/ (grep both directions); the helper's module is below the justify
   band after the change (node tools/quality/size-scan.mjs loc --host
   <module> reports under 1000 LOC) — must_fix otherwise, even when the
   issue named that module; a new helper module has a header comment;
   nothing else moved, except a runtime value the helper needs that moved
   with it out of an over-band home (the old host imports it back).
   For issues whose lens: is D8 the bar is: exactly the ratified shape landed;
   no behaviour change beyond what the ruling names; every removed mechanism's
   spec anchors are still satisfied by the new shape (read the cited clauses);
   the replaced code is gone (no dual path left); tests changed only as ruled.
   For issues whose lens: is D6 the bar is: the divergent side now matches
   the anchored/ruled posture (read both sides; open the anchor and confirm
   it is still satisfied); the diff touches failure paths only — success-path
   behaviour unchanged; no third posture invented; for text-drift the old
   spelling greps to zero live consumers (bug-doc history quoting the old
   text is fine); tests updated only as the issue or ruling names, none
   weakened; no file outside the cited sites touched.
   For issues whose lens: is D10 the bar is: for a decayed-pointer, the
   re-pointed citation resolves (open the target; the symbol/test exists
   and asserts the claimed observable) and the claim's wording is
   byte-identical; for a confirmed unwitnessed-claim, the diff ADDS a
   witness section/line naming exactly the witnesses the ## Triage ruling
   verified — no more, no fewer (open each: the file exists and carries
   the named describe/test title) — while every pre-existing record line
   is byte-identical; for a ruled reword, exactly the quoted wording landed
   and nothing else; in every shape no code or test file changed, the
   citation-form gates still pass on the edited file, and no other record
   was touched.
   For issues whose lens: is D1 the bar is: exactly the ratified shape
   landed and nothing else; behaviour identical at every former site; any
   new/changed helper module satisfies the D4 import-edge and home-band
   bars; the narrowed surface's removed fields/arms grep to zero live
   references; tests changed only as ruled.
3. Honesty. Do not take the diff's or any report's word for anything you can
   check directly.

Reply with the requested schema:
- ok: true ONLY if at least one issue is verifiably resolved by the
  working-tree changes OR at least one claimed already-resolved issue is
  confirmed against HEAD, AND there is no collateral damage. An EMPTY diff
  with at least one confirmed already-resolved issue is a valid ok outcome.
- fixed_confirmed: the issue file BASENAMES you verified resolved BY THE
  WORKING-TREE CHANGES (never list an already-resolved issue here)
- already_resolved_confirmed: the claimed basenames you verified already
  resolved at HEAD (problem gone, no attributable diff); empty when none
- already_resolved_sha: the upstream commit you verified resolved them, when
  ONE sha explains every entry above; "" when none was identifiable or they
  differ (the orchestrator renders "sha unknown")
- must_fix: "" when ok; otherwise one concrete paragraph telling the fixer
  exactly what to correct.
- unconfirmed: one clause per manifest issue you did NOT list in
  fixed_confirmed or already_resolved_confirmed — its basename and why it is not resolved (untouched; shed by
  the fixer; partial: what still remains). This is what a human reads on the
  issue when it comes back unfixed, so name the remaining copies / sites
  concretely. "" when every manifest issue is confirmed.`?
rv
